1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Simply boil water to see what it can do.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:19,360
Provide locomotion, generate electricity, clean just about anything, and power outlandish gizmos.

3
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:26,720
But if you don't watch out, it can huff, then puff, then blow your house down.

4
00:00:26,721 --> 00:00:28,820
All right, ready to go balls out?

5
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:30,200
Ready to go full steam ahead?

6
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:32,920
You'll find out where all these terms come from coming up.

7
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:37,040
Now, Steam Power on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:52,120
Steam cars.

9
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:54,820
In the early days of the automobile,

10
00:00:54,821 --> 00:00:57,780
they were more popular than the internal combustion engine.

11
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:01,520
To learn about them from an expert,

12
00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:03,460
we went to Jay Leno's garage.

13
00:01:04,260 --> 00:01:06,000
In two enormous buildings,

14
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:08,700
Jay has amassed a collection of exceptional

15
00:01:08,701 --> 00:01:10,280
and unusual motor vehicles,

16
00:01:10,780 --> 00:01:12,340
including some classic steamers.

17
00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:14,340
What we're going to do right now is fire up

18
00:01:14,341 --> 00:01:17,420
a 1909 Stanley steam car.

19
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:20,760
Steam cars are quite popular to turn of the century

20
00:01:20,761 --> 00:01:22,960
because, don't forget, cars back in the early days,

21
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:24,800
you had to crank them, and you had a magneto,

22
00:01:24,820 --> 00:01:26,980
and you had to set the choke, and a lot of times they backfire

23
00:01:26,981 --> 00:01:28,220
and people would break their arms.

24
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:30,700
Where steam...

25
00:01:30,701 --> 00:01:33,960
Well, steam technology was 100 years old by the time this car was built.

26
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:36,300
Steam technology was easily understood.

27
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:40,360
You heat up water, it makes steam, it pushes the piston, and you go.

28
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:42,320
Let's open up the hood.

29
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:50,040
Okay, not as pretty as looking at a 289 Mustang or a 327 Chevy,

30
00:01:50,041 --> 00:01:51,800
but this is not your engine.

31
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:53,140
This is just your boiler.

32
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:56,480
Getting an old steamer ready to run isn't simple.

33
00:01:57,360 --> 00:01:59,620
It's a multi-step, time-consuming process.

34
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:02,220
And firing up the boiler can be an adventure.

35
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:07,920
This is one of the few cars you can get burned to death and scalded to death at the same time.

36
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:11,800
I mean, you're carrying an open flame and you're made of wood.

37
00:02:13,780 --> 00:02:15,960
Adding air pressure? Easy.

38
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:18,740
Pilot light? No problem.

39
00:02:18,741 --> 00:02:23,940
But then Jay's first attempt to ignite the gas injectors that provide the heat for the boiler fails.

40
00:02:24,580 --> 00:02:25,420
Not good.

41
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:27,640
Okay, that's a bad sign.

42
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:37,480
Now he's not sure how much vaporized gas is floating around.

43
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:39,660
Let's take a chance.

44
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:41,380
There you go.

45
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:44,420
Exactly what I'm talking about.

46
00:02:45,220 --> 00:02:46,900
As you can see, it is burned.

47
00:02:48,740 --> 00:02:51,180
See, when you go home, your wife knows where you've been.

48
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:55,400
She can't accuse you of being out or doing something when your arm is on fire.

49
00:02:56,560 --> 00:03:00,700
Jay has better luck the second time around, and soon the boiler is warming up.

50
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:05,300
The car lets out a loud moan.

51
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:14,200
Next, Jay pushes the car outside to fill up the car's 30-gallon water tank.

52
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:18,520
I'm slowly building steam pressure. Let's see if we got any. I'll check my whistle.

53
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:22,040
I'm getting there.

54
00:03:22,520 --> 00:03:24,160
See, the nice thing about steam is

55
00:03:24,161 --> 00:03:27,580
a gasoline engine might turn 3,500 revolutions per minute.

56
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:31,420
This engine turns about 357 revolutions per mile.

57
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:34,820
It's just like that.

58
00:03:34,860 --> 00:03:36,760
So when you pull away, dead silent.

59
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:45,340
There's no gears to shift.

60
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:46,480
There's no transmission.

61
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:47,320
There's no clutch.

62
00:03:47,321 --> 00:03:50,280
Steam is so powerful you don't need gears.

63
00:03:50,281 --> 00:03:54,280
A steam engine is known as an external combustion engine

64
00:03:54,281 --> 00:03:56,280
because unlike an internal combustion engine,

65
00:03:56,281 --> 00:04:01,280
the power is produced outside of the engine in a boiler or steam generator.

66
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:06,280
When water boils, it converts from a liquid to a gas.

67
00:04:06,281 --> 00:04:10,280
It expands in the process, creating pressure in a closed system.

68
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:16,280
That pressure can be used to do work like running pistons in Jay's Stanley steamer,

69
00:04:16,281 --> 00:04:19,780
While inside a pressurized system, steam is invisible.

70
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:22,680
It's when it vents into cooler temperatures

71
00:04:22,681 --> 00:04:25,260
that it condenses into white clouds of water vapor.

72
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:28,940
This is your throttle right here.

73
00:04:29,340 --> 00:04:31,300
And you open your throttle and you go.

74
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:35,320
Let's see if we can beat the camera car.

75
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:44,600
Jay has hit 70 miles per hour in this car.

76
00:04:44,601 --> 00:04:53,880
Not bad, but the Stanley steamer land speed record is 127 miles per hour, set in 1906.

77
00:04:55,320 --> 00:04:59,620
When you drive a steam car, you learn to shut off your throttle as you approach the stop sign,

78
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:04,120
so you get a buildup of steam. You don't have limitless steam. You're actually

79
00:05:04,121 --> 00:05:09,520
using steam faster than you're making it, so you want to try to conserve as much steam as you can.

80
00:05:09,521 --> 00:05:19,600
After taking a spin in his Stanley, Jay decides to roll out one of his more elegant steamers,

81
00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:22,720
a 1907 white steam car.

82
00:05:23,780 --> 00:05:28,540
This was a bit more expensive, a bit more prestigious than the Stanley steam car.

83
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:29,480
This was a step up.

84
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:31,580
They built 10,000 of these.

85
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:33,600
This was the first White House car.

86
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:36,200
Teddy Roosevelt rode in a white steamer.

87
00:05:36,201 --> 00:05:41,880
Starting up this car doesn't take as long because of its more efficient way of producing

88
00:05:41,881 --> 00:05:42,880
steam.

89
00:05:42,881 --> 00:05:45,980
With the Stanley, you're heating up 15 gallons of water.

90
00:05:45,981 --> 00:05:48,860
You have a big fire, like a giant kettle.

91
00:05:48,861 --> 00:05:51,760
With this, you're never heating more than two quarts of water at a time.

92
00:05:51,761 --> 00:05:59,420
As you can see, the white was a lot more relaxed version of the Stanley.

93
00:05:59,421 --> 00:06:02,320
Not quite as hectic.

94
00:06:02,321 --> 00:06:04,320
Big comfortable cars.

95
00:06:04,321 --> 00:06:07,080
35 miles an hour is a nice cruising speed.

96
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:09,940
This is what they call a condensing car.

97
00:06:10,020 --> 00:06:11,260
If you notice, unlike the Stanley,

98
00:06:11,340 --> 00:06:13,760
you don't see plumes of steam coming out the back

99
00:06:13,761 --> 00:06:15,920
because the steam is used by the engine.

100
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:18,860
It goes back up into the condenser,

101
00:06:18,940 --> 00:06:20,920
which looks like a radiator, but it's a condenser.

102
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:23,640
The cool air from the fan cools the steam,

103
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:27,240
turns it into water, and you use it over and over again.

104
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:29,600
I mean, eventually it dissipates,

105
00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:31,740
whereas the Stanley, you might get only

106
00:06:31,741 --> 00:06:34,560
a mile and a half on a gallon of water,

107
00:06:34,920 --> 00:06:37,660
and this you might get five miles on a gallon of water.

108
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:40,400
The ride may have been quiet and comfortable,

109
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:43,980
but the lower sticker prices on gasoline-powered cars,

110
00:06:44,340 --> 00:06:47,120
along with a quicker starting internal combustion engine,

111
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:50,240
caused steamer sales to fizzle.

112
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:54,880
Gasoline cars could do it quicker, better, faster.

113
00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:57,180
And that's really the name of the game.

114
00:06:57,500 --> 00:07:00,380
I love them, but if your wife's pregnant,

115
00:07:00,381 --> 00:07:01,320
You need to get to the hospital.

116
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:02,940
You can't be out there with a match.

117
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:04,780
I'll be out there about another 10 minutes on it.

118
00:07:04,980 --> 00:07:06,040
It's not going to work.

119
00:07:07,980 --> 00:07:11,060
Decades before steam cars and for decades after their fall,

120
00:07:11,520 --> 00:07:13,300
steam locomotives ruled the rails.

121
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:20,220
In 1804, English inventor Richard Trevithick unveiled a steam locomotive capable of hauling 10 tons.

122
00:07:24,060 --> 00:07:29,060
They quickly grew in size and power and became important workhorses in the Industrial Revolution.

123
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:35,320
Throughout the 1800s, the steam locomotive played a major role in the settlement of the

124
00:07:35,321 --> 00:07:37,320
American West.

125
00:07:37,321 --> 00:07:42,440
A defining moment occurred in 1869 when the completion of the Transcontinental Railroad

126
00:07:42,441 --> 00:07:47,440
linked the United States from coast to coast.

127
00:07:47,441 --> 00:07:53,260
In remote Eli, Nevada, the Nevada Northern Railway houses its historic steam locomotives

128
00:07:53,261 --> 00:07:55,620
in their original engine house.

129
00:07:55,621 --> 00:08:00,620
The locomotive we're going to take out today is locomotive 93.

130
00:08:00,621 --> 00:08:05,620
She was purchased by the railroad in July of 1909.

131
00:08:05,621 --> 00:08:10,620
Right now the fireman is preparing the fire to produce the steam.

132
00:08:12,620 --> 00:08:17,620
What I'm doing now is I'm shoveling coal into the firebox,

133
00:08:17,621 --> 00:08:21,620
and we cover the grates evenly with fire

134
00:08:21,621 --> 00:08:28,620
and to build the pressure up to our operating pressure, about 185 psi.

135
00:08:31,620 --> 00:08:36,620
This firebox is big. It's about 12 feet deep and about 6 feet wide.

136
00:08:36,621 --> 00:08:40,620
This engine takes a lot of coal to make it run.

137
00:08:40,621 --> 00:08:44,620
It's an incredibly labor-intensive machine.

138
00:08:44,621 --> 00:08:49,620
Right now, we're lubing it, getting it ready to go out on the line.

139
00:08:51,620 --> 00:08:54,420
Once the boiler builds up enough pressure,

140
00:08:54,421 --> 00:08:56,960
steam generates electricity for the train's lights

141
00:08:56,961 --> 00:08:59,700
and runs the air pumps on the front of the locomotive

142
00:08:59,701 --> 00:09:03,940
that produce compressed air for braking and other functions.

143
00:09:03,941 --> 00:09:06,000
Then up goes the engine house door,

144
00:09:06,001 --> 00:09:08,540
and locomotive 93 slowly chugs out.

145
00:09:21,620 --> 00:09:26,380
We just received the highball, releasing the engine brake.

146
00:09:26,381 --> 00:09:30,740
Placing the power reverse and forward.

147
00:09:31,620 --> 00:09:32,720
And pull it out on the throttle.

148
00:09:33,080 --> 00:09:34,140
And two blasts on the whistle.

149
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:36,860
And off we go.

150
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:44,460
This is copper mining country.

151
00:09:44,940 --> 00:09:49,720
And for nearly 50 years, locomotive number 93's job was to haul ore cars.

152
00:09:49,721 --> 00:09:51,520
This is a freight locomotive.

153
00:09:51,521 --> 00:09:53,320
You can tell by the smaller wheels.

154
00:09:53,321 --> 00:09:56,120
It has a lot of track of effort, but very little horsepower.

155
00:09:56,121 --> 00:09:58,600
Track of effort is about 42,000 pounds,

156
00:09:58,601 --> 00:10:01,840
but the horsepower is only 905.

157
00:10:01,841 --> 00:10:03,800
It's built for power, not for speed.

158
00:10:03,801 --> 00:10:06,400
Used to pull about 33 or cars up the hill.

159
00:10:10,280 --> 00:10:11,880
Since this is a working locomotive,

160
00:10:11,881 --> 00:10:13,400
you got to be careful to let you touch.

161
00:10:13,401 --> 00:10:14,520
Everything's extremely hot.

162
00:10:14,521 --> 00:10:15,360
It's hot.

163
00:10:18,820 --> 00:10:21,700
Before they return number 93 to the engine house,

164
00:10:21,701 --> 00:10:24,400
the crew performs a boiler cleaning procedure

165
00:10:24,401 --> 00:10:27,100
that gives just a hint of the amount of pressurized steam

166
00:10:27,101 --> 00:10:29,440
inside.

167
00:10:29,441 --> 00:10:32,340
After each run, we do what we call a blowdown.

168
00:10:32,341 --> 00:10:34,840
We do this because sediments build up in the boiler,

169
00:10:34,841 --> 00:10:37,040
and we have to clean the boiler out,

170
00:10:37,041 --> 00:10:38,720
get those sediments out of the boiler.

171
00:10:38,721 --> 00:10:39,540
So here goes.

172
00:10:44,520 --> 00:10:53,600
In the 19th century, the steam engine revolutionized how we live and ushered in the dawn of a new age of transportation.

173
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:59,600
A trip from New York to Philadelphia that used to take two days now took only two hours.

174
00:11:00,540 --> 00:11:07,040
Farmers could ship their products to distant markets, as the speed of railroads met crops wouldn't spoil before being sold.

175
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:12,700
But it's not just locomotives that demonstrate the awesome power of steam.

176
00:11:12,701 --> 00:11:19,300
At the Nevada Northern Railway Museum, this 100-ton steam crane is called the Big Hook.

177
00:11:20,420 --> 00:11:26,340
Built in 1907, its main purpose was to clear train wrecks and place derailed cars back on the tracks.

178
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:34,140
This steam-powered crane is similar to a steam locomotive, except that we're not running wheels on steel rails.

179
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:41,240
What we're running here are large gears that pick up the boom and also the two hooks in the front.

180
00:11:42,700 --> 00:11:47,080
Before it can pick up a derailed car, the crane's outriggers must be extended.

181
00:11:47,081 --> 00:11:51,900
Then the crew places wood blocks underneath each to stabilize the crane.

182
00:11:51,901 --> 00:11:53,540
Now the heavy lifting can begin.

183
00:11:56,360 --> 00:12:00,780
Next we're going to swing the crane over to the load we're going to pick up.

184
00:12:00,781 --> 00:12:04,080
And to do that, I have to engage the swing hook.

185
00:12:04,081 --> 00:12:06,640
And then I have to put a direction on it.

186
00:12:06,641 --> 00:12:11,320
And then I will apply steam, which is powered by these levers.

187
00:12:11,321 --> 00:12:12,320
And here we go.

188
00:12:17,820 --> 00:12:21,540
Next we're gonna lower the hooks to pick up the load,

189
00:12:21,541 --> 00:12:22,760
which would mean that I engage,

190
00:12:22,761 --> 00:12:25,960
first I engage this one, this one here, this clutch.

191
00:12:28,680 --> 00:12:30,000
That's it's engaged.

192
00:12:30,001 --> 00:12:31,440
Now we're gonna let her down.

193
00:12:33,540 --> 00:12:35,340
The big hook may look huge,

194
00:12:35,341 --> 00:12:37,640
and it's brawny enough to lift up this ore car.

195
00:12:37,641 --> 00:12:44,100
But it's nothing in size compared to some of the largest steam-powered machines ever constructed.

196
00:12:45,360 --> 00:12:51,500
The great ocean liners of the 19th and 20th centuries, which changed travel and transportation forever.

197
00:12:52,800 --> 00:12:57,480
The Titanic was the largest when it sank on its maiden voyage in 1912.

198
00:12:58,240 --> 00:13:04,100
But the RMS Queen Elizabeth, launched in 1938, was the biggest steamship ever built.

199
00:13:04,101 --> 00:13:10,100
We know the smaller vessels designed to navigate rivers and lakes as steamboats.

200
00:13:10,101 --> 00:13:16,500
It was in New York in 1807 that Robert Fulton's Claremont offered the first successful commercial

201
00:13:16,501 --> 00:13:19,060
steamboat service in the world.

202
00:13:19,061 --> 00:13:24,620
Today, there are only a few working steamboats in America.

203
00:13:24,621 --> 00:13:31,840
In New Orleans, the Natchez provides a reminder of the golden age of steamboating on the Mississippi.

204
00:13:31,841 --> 00:13:37,500
While the boat is a recreation built in 1975, the enormous steam engines are hand-me-downs

205
00:13:37,501 --> 00:13:40,680
from a bygone era.

206
00:13:40,681 --> 00:13:44,840
These engines that you see right here were built in 1925.

207
00:13:44,841 --> 00:13:49,315
They were built for the steamboat Clarity, which was a pushboat for the U.S.

208
00:13:49,316 --> 00:13:50,080
Steel Company

209
00:13:50,081 --> 00:13:51,080
in Pennsylvania.

210
00:13:51,081 --> 00:13:57,860
It wasn't unusual for steamboats to get machinery from the previous vessel.

211
00:13:57,861 --> 00:14:00,260
The wooden boats only lasted a short time,

212
00:14:00,261 --> 00:14:02,340
and the machinery would quite often be removed

213
00:14:02,341 --> 00:14:05,400
from one older vessel onto the new one.

214
00:14:05,401 --> 00:14:08,160
Both engines work in tandem to move the huge arms

215
00:14:08,161 --> 00:14:11,980
that turn the 26-ton oak and steel paddle wheel.

216
00:14:11,981 --> 00:14:14,000
The total maximum output of the two engines

217
00:14:14,001 --> 00:14:18,680
is equivalent to about 1,600 horsepower.

218
00:14:18,681 --> 00:14:20,280
The boilers that supply the steam

219
00:14:20,281 --> 00:14:22,620
aren't vintage equipment because of safety,

220
00:14:22,621 --> 00:14:25,380
economic, and environmental reasons.

221
00:14:25,381 --> 00:14:26,600
The old boats use coal.

222
00:14:27,240 --> 00:14:28,900
Unlike us, we're using diesel fuel.

223
00:14:29,600 --> 00:14:30,400
There are two boilers.

224
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:31,920
We only use one at a time.

225
00:14:33,340 --> 00:14:35,880
Not all the steam produced heads right to the engines.

226
00:14:36,540 --> 00:14:38,780
One small pipe goes up to the top of the boat.

227
00:14:42,560 --> 00:14:45,780
The few boats that are in service now have a very distinctive whistle.

228
00:14:46,280 --> 00:14:48,920
Most steamboat people can automatically hear them

229
00:14:48,921 --> 00:14:50,480
and know what exact boat they're from.

230
00:14:51,320 --> 00:14:54,560
A whistle isn't the only way to get people's attention on this boat.

231
00:14:55,380 --> 00:14:59,220
Another pipe runs across the top deck to supply steam to the calliope.

232
00:15:00,380 --> 00:15:05,420
First thing you have to do is clear the condensation that's built up in the whistles.

233
00:15:06,600 --> 00:15:11,920
We have 32 keys on our keyboard. Each key corresponds to a steam whistle.

234
00:15:12,700 --> 00:15:15,322
The steam organ was patented by Joshua C.

235
00:15:15,323 --> 00:15:18,200
Stoddard of Worcester, Massachusetts in 1856.

236
00:15:19,220 --> 00:15:20,999
His brother was a riverboat captain.

237
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,420
He gave it to his brother who put it on the boat,

238
00:15:23,421 --> 00:15:26,660
and it became a principal means of advertising

239
00:15:26,661 --> 00:15:30,880
and welcoming passengers aboard steamboats by the late 1850s.

240
00:15:31,300 --> 00:15:35,000
It was later named Calliope for the Greek news of voice.

241
00:15:36,880 --> 00:15:40,560
I'm going to close this act with a theme you should all recognize.

242
00:15:48,380 --> 00:15:52,160
Trains, boats and steam cars created a more mobile world.

243
00:15:52,161 --> 00:15:58,160
But just as important was steam's role in the factories of the Industrial Revolution.

244
00:15:58,161 --> 00:16:05,160
Jay Leno decided to go back 150 years and see what some old but proven technology had to offer.

245
00:16:05,161 --> 00:16:12,160
He acquired a Wright stationary steam engine, built in the 1860s, and installed it in his huge automotive garage.

246
00:16:12,161 --> 00:16:15,160
Abraham Lincoln was president when this thing was designed.

247
00:16:15,161 --> 00:16:21,160
It weighs 11 tons. This flywheel here is 3 tons. It's built in two pieces.

248
00:16:21,161 --> 00:16:25,160
but the rest of it is all one casting.

249
00:16:25,161 --> 00:16:28,160
Of course, if you decide to buy a steam engine,

250
00:16:28,161 --> 00:16:30,160
you also need a source of steam.

251
00:16:30,161 --> 00:16:33,160
We've got a modern Clayton steam generator.

252
00:16:33,161 --> 00:16:34,160
It's a little complicated.

253
00:16:34,161 --> 00:16:35,160
Bernard has the cheat sheet.

254
00:16:35,161 --> 00:16:37,160
Get the cheat sheet, Bernard.

255
00:16:37,161 --> 00:16:39,160
We always want to make sure we fire this thing right up properly

256
00:16:39,161 --> 00:16:42,160
because there's a lot of valves to open and things you have to do.

257
00:16:42,161 --> 00:16:44,160
Well, first step, we've got to check the water

258
00:16:44,161 --> 00:16:46,160
and the header tank up there.

259
00:16:46,161 --> 00:16:48,160
The header tank looks good. We got water in the header tank.

260
00:16:48,161 --> 00:16:49,160
We got it on, okay.

261
00:16:49,161 --> 00:16:53,660
Next, Jay and Bernard open a series of valves to get the water flowing through the system.

262
00:16:54,240 --> 00:16:56,380
So that's starting to look pretty good there.

263
00:16:56,460 --> 00:17:00,680
So let's push the fill run button over there and we'll get some fire going.

264
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:05,520
Now we're waiting for steam to come out of that pipe up on top.

265
00:17:07,640 --> 00:17:09,000
There she blows, Captain.

266
00:17:11,260 --> 00:17:13,440
Now that we got steam, let's go back inside.

267
00:17:14,140 --> 00:17:18,400
Luckily, this thing operates on natural gas, so I don't have to shovel coal all day.

268
00:17:19,160 --> 00:17:20,160
That wouldn't do it.

269
00:17:22,280 --> 00:17:25,420
As you can see, we've got almost 140 pounds of steam pressure,

270
00:17:25,520 --> 00:17:27,640
much more than this would have run on at the time.

271
00:17:28,080 --> 00:17:31,260
So let's open our valve here and see what happens.

272
00:17:31,420 --> 00:17:33,340
And everything starts moving.

273
00:17:34,580 --> 00:17:36,920
I still get a kick out of this each time it starts.

274
00:17:37,040 --> 00:17:37,980
It's always amazing to me.

275
00:17:38,320 --> 00:17:39,640
You know, we're so used to...

276
00:17:39,641 --> 00:17:41,480
And machines starting machines.

277
00:17:41,580 --> 00:17:42,980
The fact that this runs so quietly.

278
00:17:42,981 --> 00:17:50,440
One reason this 11-ton single-piston right steam engine does run so quietly

279
00:17:50,441 --> 00:17:55,200
is that Jay installed a 7-ton perfectly level cement base for the engine to rest on.

280
00:17:56,520 --> 00:18:01,000
If you're a little off, your piston will eat into the bore and wear your cylinder out.

281
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:06,060
So that's the real trick, making sure everything is level and smooth and moves freely.

282
00:18:07,140 --> 00:18:09,800
So far, Jay hasn't put his steam engine to work.

283
00:18:10,720 --> 00:18:17,720
Back in the steam age, the rotating flywheel would run a big belt to transfer its power to saws and other mill or factory machinery.

284
00:18:19,760 --> 00:18:21,740
This is what the Luddites were afraid of.

285
00:18:21,780 --> 00:18:24,420
You all heard the expression, Luddites, people don't like technology.

286
00:18:24,760 --> 00:18:29,580
But you realize a machine like this would probably replace 100 men working.

287
00:18:30,040 --> 00:18:33,860
It was 125 horsepower, which doesn't seem like much now.

288
00:18:33,960 --> 00:18:36,240
We have engines this big to make 125 horsepower.

289
00:18:36,241 --> 00:18:40,280
But back in the day, this is almost like space shuttle technology.

290
00:18:41,140 --> 00:18:44,640
What makes old steam engines fascinating in this age of electronics

291
00:18:44,641 --> 00:18:49,440
are the rhythmic sounds and the enormous moving parts,

292
00:18:50,320 --> 00:18:52,660
a potential hazard in 19th century factories.

293
00:18:53,720 --> 00:18:55,660
There was no OSHA back in the day.

294
00:18:55,740 --> 00:18:57,960
There was no health and safety standards.

295
00:18:58,080 --> 00:18:59,400
The guy would go, hey, I'll fix it.

296
00:18:59,820 --> 00:19:01,520
And that was pretty much the end of it.

297
00:19:01,521 --> 00:19:06,520
Next, Jay starts up his even older 1832 steam engine.

298
00:19:06,521 --> 00:19:09,520
Give it a little bit of a push here to get it going.

299
00:19:09,521 --> 00:19:11,520
Get it over center.

300
00:19:11,521 --> 00:19:13,520
And there she goes.

301
00:19:15,520 --> 00:19:18,520
As you can see, there's kind of a quiet elegance to these things.

302
00:19:18,521 --> 00:19:20,520
This is what they call a walking beam,

303
00:19:20,521 --> 00:19:23,520
because it's a beam and it looks like it's walking.

304
00:19:23,521 --> 00:19:25,520
It's pretty self-explanatory.

305
00:19:25,521 --> 00:19:30,520
This ran a color and dye factory in England back in the early 1800s.

306
00:19:30,521 --> 00:19:34,260
And I'm told this engine ran for almost 100 years continuously.

307
00:19:34,520 --> 00:19:37,400
Just like this. Chug, chug, chug. That's what it did.

308
00:19:38,420 --> 00:19:40,460
The engine isn't entirely original,

309
00:19:40,680 --> 00:19:44,320
but there's a good reason the oilers were added later to make lubrication easier.

310
00:19:45,480 --> 00:19:48,280
This is 1832. How did they lubricate them?

311
00:19:48,520 --> 00:19:52,120
What you'd do is you'd bring a pig in, they'd slaughter a pig,

312
00:19:52,220 --> 00:19:56,260
they'd rub the greasy parts all over the metal, run it for a while.

313
00:19:56,340 --> 00:19:58,220
Hey, we need another pig. Bring another pig in.

314
00:19:58,221 --> 00:20:00,460
They'd slaughter another pig, rub the greasy parts all over,

315
00:20:00,660 --> 00:20:02,520
and probably ate a lot of pork chops.

316
00:20:02,840 --> 00:20:03,760
That's probably what they did.

317
00:20:03,840 --> 00:20:05,080
Luckily, we don't do that anymore.

318
00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:09,740
Centrifugal force causes these circulating balls to flare outward.

319
00:20:10,640 --> 00:20:12,340
They're the engine's main safety feature.

320
00:20:13,260 --> 00:20:15,680
The faster you go, the higher this rises,

321
00:20:16,160 --> 00:20:18,580
which moves that lever, which controls your throttle.

322
00:20:19,040 --> 00:20:20,660
You hear the expression, balls out.

323
00:20:21,180 --> 00:20:22,760
It doesn't mean what you think, okay?

324
00:20:23,080 --> 00:20:25,040
What it means is your ball's running all the way out,

325
00:20:25,220 --> 00:20:26,880
your engine is running at full speed,

326
00:20:26,881 --> 00:20:29,460
and you keep it from over-revving it and breaking your motor,

327
00:20:29,900 --> 00:20:31,420
it essentially works as a governor.

328
00:20:31,640 --> 00:20:34,180
It limits how much throttle you can put into it.

329
00:20:35,200 --> 00:20:36,960
T's engines may be antiques,

330
00:20:37,180 --> 00:20:39,420
but steam is still a vital source of power.

331
00:20:41,220 --> 00:20:44,680
All your nuclear subs run on steam.

332
00:20:45,620 --> 00:20:48,860
Steam turbines run a lot of power plants.

333
00:20:49,760 --> 00:20:52,260
Steam is still a force to be reckoned with.

334
00:20:52,261 --> 00:20:57,260
Coal, natural gas, and nuclear energy all create steam.

335
00:20:57,261 --> 00:21:03,260
But there's a more environmentally friendly way to turn the turbines that generate electricity.

336
00:21:03,261 --> 00:21:11,260
Yellowstone National Park spurting and gushing geysers are examples of the volatile subterranean interaction of heat and water.

337
00:21:11,261 --> 00:21:15,260
Another is the biggest steam explosion in U.S. history.

338
00:21:15,261 --> 00:21:22,260
The cataclysmic eruption of Mount St. Helens in Washington State on May 18, 1980.

339
00:21:22,261 --> 00:21:31,400
Inside the volcano, water hit superheated rock and flashed into steam with the explosive force of about 20 million tons of TNT.

340
00:21:32,220 --> 00:21:37,060
The blast devastated 150 square miles of forest in six minutes.

341
00:21:38,060 --> 00:21:40,240
That's the power of steam uncontrolled.

342
00:21:41,040 --> 00:21:44,760
Elsewhere, that natural power is being harnessed to produce electricity.

343
00:21:45,280 --> 00:21:49,700
The first geothermal power plants in the U.S. were built in 1962.

344
00:21:49,701 --> 00:21:53,740
at the Geysers Dry Steam Field, located in Northern California.

345
00:21:54,780 --> 00:21:58,060
It's still the largest producing geothermal field in the world,

346
00:21:58,520 --> 00:22:02,540
using high-temperature dry steam as the resource to generate electricity.

347
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:10,400
In dry steam power plants, the steam shoots up from the wells and is used to turn turbines,

348
00:22:11,100 --> 00:22:14,080
while most other geothermal plants, called flash plants,

349
00:22:14,520 --> 00:22:17,760
use naturally occurring superheated water as a source of steam.

350
00:22:19,700 --> 00:22:27,340
In Southern California's Imperial Valley, along the shore of the Salton Sea, the Cal

351
00:22:27,341 --> 00:22:33,820
Energy Company currently has 10 flash plants, all situated over a hotbed of geothermal activity.

352
00:22:33,821 --> 00:22:39,560
On those 10 plants, we're generating 340 megawatts of power, which equates to about

353
00:22:39,561 --> 00:22:42,820
333,000 homes that it powers.

354
00:22:42,821 --> 00:22:49,540
We sell on long-term power contracts to utilities throughout Southern California and Arizona.

355
00:22:49,541 --> 00:22:52,760
Just like the Salton Sea above, the water below the ground

356
00:22:52,761 --> 00:22:54,480
is very salty.

357
00:22:54,481 --> 00:22:55,800
It's referred to as brine.

358
00:22:58,360 --> 00:23:01,720
Geothermal production wells, similar to oil wells,

359
00:23:01,721 --> 00:23:03,920
drill down thousands of feet to tap

360
00:23:03,921 --> 00:23:08,060
into the more than 360-degree Fahrenheit reservoirs of brine

361
00:23:08,061 --> 00:23:11,120
trapped in the bedrock.

362
00:23:11,121 --> 00:23:13,580
Tremendous pressure causes the superheated brine

363
00:23:13,581 --> 00:23:14,720
to rush to the surface.

364
00:23:14,721 --> 00:23:19,720
This is Vonderay 1 production well.

365
00:23:19,721 --> 00:23:25,220
Vonderay 1 was recognized by the World Geothermal Resources Council as being the most prolific

366
00:23:25,221 --> 00:23:27,220
geothermal well in the world.

367
00:23:27,221 --> 00:23:29,960
Don't let all that noise scare you.

368
00:23:29,961 --> 00:23:35,360
These yellow bars are actually restraints because the well head was moving so much and

369
00:23:35,361 --> 00:23:40,160
it was a little too prolific.

370
00:23:40,161 --> 00:23:44,820
the hot brine from the wells flows through pipes into large cylindrical tanks.

371
00:23:44,821 --> 00:23:50,440
This part of the process is why this type of geothermal plant is called a flash plant.

372
00:23:50,441 --> 00:23:56,100
As the pressure is lowered inside the vessel, steam is flashed similar to what you would

373
00:23:56,101 --> 00:23:59,120
see in a pressure cooker in your home.

374
00:23:59,121 --> 00:24:04,400
As the steam is flashed, it's routed from the top of the vessel, the brine water that

375
00:24:04,401 --> 00:24:10,980
remains is exiting on the bottom half so that there is no contamination between the two.

376
00:24:11,980 --> 00:24:17,380
The steam without salts is then routed to turn the blades inside the turbines that generate electricity.

377
00:24:20,260 --> 00:24:25,340
While the steam is used to turn the turbines, the remaining liquid that didn't flash into steam

378
00:24:25,341 --> 00:24:31,540
is pumped away from the plants to injection wells that recycle it back into the underground reservoirs.

379
00:24:31,541 --> 00:24:37,020
We re-inject 80% of all the fluid that we pull out in the production system,

380
00:24:37,140 --> 00:24:38,980
so we're self-sustaining the reservoir.

381
00:24:40,720 --> 00:24:45,760
Geologists estimate there's approximately 2,000 megawatts of power still untapped in the area,

382
00:24:46,260 --> 00:24:49,120
enough energy to supply about 2 million more homes.

383
00:24:51,900 --> 00:24:54,380
They're the heavy lifters and big movers,

384
00:24:54,680 --> 00:24:57,580
but they get gunky and grimy and need to be clean.

385
00:24:58,300 --> 00:24:59,000
What can you do?

386
00:24:59,001 --> 00:25:02,780
The Sioux Corporation of South Dakota has the answer.

387
00:25:02,781 --> 00:25:05,120
Roll out one of its steam cleaning units,

388
00:25:05,121 --> 00:25:11,220
attach a water supply, fire up the boiler, and blast away.

389
00:25:11,221 --> 00:25:19,440
Steam is just a fantastic way to remove grease and dirt

390
00:25:19,441 --> 00:25:21,140
from heavy equipment.

391
00:25:21,141 --> 00:25:24,040
This steam cleaning unit has a diesel tank

392
00:25:24,041 --> 00:25:25,880
that supplies fuel to the burner.

393
00:25:25,881 --> 00:25:28,880
The upper portion is called a water tube boiler.

394
00:25:28,881 --> 00:25:34,880
Hot air produced by the burners below circulates around two coils of water-filled steel pipe,

395
00:25:34,881 --> 00:25:38,880
up through the center, down between the coils, then up the outside,

396
00:25:38,881 --> 00:25:41,880
before exhausting out the top of the unit.

397
00:25:41,881 --> 00:25:48,880
To build one of these coils, we take 200 feet of pipe and wind it on a drum into a cylinder,

398
00:25:48,881 --> 00:25:53,880
and then we wind a smaller one for the inner cylinder of another 100 feet

399
00:25:53,881 --> 00:25:58,560
for a total of 300 feet of pipe that's all wound together and welded together

400
00:25:58,561 --> 00:26:01,260
to have one continuous pass of 300 feet.

401
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,640
When the water passes through this 300 feet of pipe,

402
00:26:04,641 --> 00:26:08,980
it starts out at 60 degrees Fahrenheit and ends up at 325 degrees Fahrenheit.

403
00:26:09,840 --> 00:26:12,980
The hot pressurized water flows out of the boiler through the hose

404
00:26:12,981 --> 00:26:15,140
and discharges from the steam nozzle.

405
00:26:16,380 --> 00:26:19,960
These machines are designed so that only 15% of the water

406
00:26:19,961 --> 00:26:22,560
flashes into steam as it exits the nozzle.

407
00:26:23,880 --> 00:26:29,040
The 85% water, 15% steam ratio provides the best cleaning power.

408
00:26:29,760 --> 00:26:33,380
The tremendous expansion of the steam accelerates the water droplets

409
00:26:33,381 --> 00:26:36,520
so that they bombard the surface to be cleaned.

410
00:26:37,560 --> 00:26:42,000
Pressure washers that just use hot water aren't as effective when dealing with grease and grime,

411
00:26:42,220 --> 00:26:46,500
and 100% steam would melt the grease but not wash it away.

412
00:26:47,200 --> 00:26:51,980
In addition to cleaning heavy equipment like this, there are other uses for steam cleaners,

413
00:26:51,981 --> 00:26:54,560
such as blasting away snow and ice.

414
00:26:54,900 --> 00:26:58,500
It'll clean gum, sticky substances off of sidewalks.

415
00:26:58,800 --> 00:27:00,640
It can be used to clean just about anything.

416
00:27:01,720 --> 00:27:06,460
The Sioux Corporation manufactures both steam cleaning and steam generating machines.

417
00:27:07,020 --> 00:27:09,740
The heart of each machine is a reliable boiler.

418
00:27:10,620 --> 00:27:15,260
Workers make so-called fire tube boilers to go inside the company's steam generators.

419
00:27:16,160 --> 00:27:19,420
Unlike their water tube boiler, in a fire tube boiler,

420
00:27:19,421 --> 00:27:24,460
the heat from the burners travels through the tubes and boils the water inside the tank.

421
00:27:25,100 --> 00:27:29,560
And you have it filled with water to this level about approximately here.

422
00:27:29,980 --> 00:27:32,160
And the water boils just like in a tea kettle.

423
00:27:32,520 --> 00:27:34,880
And the steam comes out the exhaust right here.

424
00:27:35,440 --> 00:27:38,520
We're testing our SF-20 boiler right now.

425
00:27:39,320 --> 00:27:40,800
You'll see the steam start pouring out.

426
00:27:41,980 --> 00:27:45,320
This machine will put out about 700 pounds per hour of steam.

427
00:27:45,321 --> 00:27:52,320
It's running at a temperature of about 240 degrees right now.

428
00:27:52,321 --> 00:27:56,320
This is the discharge of our steam generator.

429
00:27:56,321 --> 00:28:01,320
When it boils inside the steam generator, it expands at a rate of 1,500 times.

430
00:28:01,321 --> 00:28:09,320
So if we start off with one cubic foot of water, we're going to have 1,500 cubic feet of steam at the discharge.

431
00:28:09,321 --> 00:28:16,500
Low-pressure steam generators can be used to accelerate concrete curing and even sterilize soil.

432
00:28:17,500 --> 00:28:19,780
But what have you got dirty clothes and wrinkles?

433
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:21,700
Steam again.

434
00:28:22,220 --> 00:28:29,760
There are about 30,000 dry cleaners in the United States, including perfect cleaners in Los Angeles, and they all rely on steam.

435
00:28:30,180 --> 00:28:36,120
Here at this large 3,500-square-foot dry cleaning plant, we process over 5,000 pieces a week.

436
00:28:36,121 --> 00:28:40,020
and every single one of them comes into contact with steam in one form or another.

437
00:28:42,200 --> 00:28:47,540
The maze of white pipes carries steam to the various devices throughout the plant that spew it out.

438
00:28:49,600 --> 00:28:51,980
All the steam comes from the boiler in the back.

439
00:28:53,260 --> 00:28:55,560
Here we are in the boiler room, the heart of the dry cleaning plant.

440
00:28:55,561 --> 00:28:58,180
As you can see, I've taken the cover off my boiler here,

441
00:28:58,420 --> 00:29:02,880
and you see the natural grass flaming and heating these water tubes, creating steam and pressure,

442
00:29:03,260 --> 00:29:05,960
carrying pressure up to 110 pounds per square inch.

443
00:29:06,120 --> 00:29:10,120
Some of the washers and dryers use steam.

444
00:29:10,121 --> 00:29:15,840
But when it comes to getting out wrinkles, steam's heat and humidity have no equal in

445
00:29:15,841 --> 00:29:18,120
getting the kinks out of cloth.

446
00:29:18,121 --> 00:29:22,120
This is a steam air form finisher, otherwise known as a Suzie in the dry cleaning business.

447
00:29:22,121 --> 00:29:26,300
I'm going to put this wrinkled polo shirt on here, push the button, we're going to have

448
00:29:26,301 --> 00:29:29,160
hot steam come out of there and then hot air to dry it.

449
00:29:29,161 --> 00:29:32,600
This is the first step in the dry cleaning pressing process before it goes over to a

450
00:29:32,601 --> 00:29:34,600
utility press.

451
00:29:34,601 --> 00:29:41,600
Utility presses emit steam from both the top and bottom inner surfaces.

452
00:29:41,601 --> 00:29:45,600
Workers also have steam irons available to target specific areas.

453
00:29:45,601 --> 00:29:49,600
This is our largest press, otherwise known as a hothead press.

454
00:29:49,601 --> 00:29:51,600
This large metal surface is filled with steam,

455
00:29:51,601 --> 00:29:54,600
gets up to a temperature of 300 plus degrees.

456
00:29:54,601 --> 00:29:57,600
It is dry though. When she brings the press down,

457
00:29:57,601 --> 00:30:00,600
she'll press this wet sheet here with some bottom steam,

458
00:30:00,601 --> 00:30:04,520
and it'll give the sheet a nice crisp finish without using any starch.

459
00:30:07,880 --> 00:30:11,120
Wrinkles aren't the only laundry problem that steam can obliterate.

460
00:30:12,040 --> 00:30:16,760
We use here steam to remove stains, and this is how we do it.

461
00:30:17,320 --> 00:30:22,280
I put on the spotting board, take my spotting gun, which shoots steam through it,

462
00:30:22,281 --> 00:30:25,560
and I can use a variety of distance depending on the garment,

463
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:29,900
so I don't damage the fabric or the color on the garment.

464
00:30:30,600 --> 00:30:38,280
The steam is allowing me to remove the steam as I go.

465
00:30:38,281 --> 00:30:44,260
While one small boiler can supply the steam for this cleaning business, in New York, enormous

466
00:30:44,261 --> 00:30:49,020
boilers send steam surging out of the city's streets and into some of the world's most

467
00:30:49,021 --> 00:30:51,320
famous skyscrapers.

468
00:30:51,321 --> 00:30:56,600
Unless things go wrong.

469
00:30:56,601 --> 00:31:01,420
What do the Empire State Building, the Chrysler Building, the United Nations, along with hospitals

470
00:31:01,421 --> 00:31:05,040
and hundreds of other Manhattan businesses have in common?

471
00:31:05,041 --> 00:31:09,420
Instead of having big boilers in their basements, they receive steam from one of Consolidated

472
00:31:09,421 --> 00:31:15,040
Edison Company's six steam generation stations, the largest steam system in the world.

473
00:31:15,041 --> 00:31:21,020
Over the course of a year, they're putting 30 billion pounds of steam into the Con Ed

474
00:31:21,021 --> 00:31:22,320
steam system.

475
00:31:22,321 --> 00:31:25,080
sent out into 105 miles of underground pipe

476
00:31:25,081 --> 00:31:27,660
throughout Manhattan, going from the tip of Manhattan

477
00:31:27,661 --> 00:31:29,440
up to 96th Street.

478
00:31:29,441 --> 00:31:33,820
We service nearly 1,800 customers.

479
00:31:33,821 --> 00:31:36,540
The 74th Street Con Ed steam generating plant

480
00:31:36,541 --> 00:31:40,940
alone puts out about 2 million pounds of steam per hour.

481
00:31:40,941 --> 00:31:43,560
We have three high pressure boilers here at 74th Street.

482
00:31:43,561 --> 00:31:46,420
And we create a fireball inside the boiler, which heats up

483
00:31:46,421 --> 00:31:51,600
60,000 gallons per hour of water, which goes up to the drum.

484
00:31:51,601 --> 00:31:53,600
that flashes into steam.

485
00:31:53,601 --> 00:31:55,360
Each of our three high pressure boilers

486
00:31:55,361 --> 00:31:56,800
has two steam drums.

487
00:31:56,801 --> 00:31:59,940
They produce approximately 450,000 pounds of steam

488
00:31:59,941 --> 00:32:03,100
per hour at a temperature of 800 degrees.

489
00:32:03,101 --> 00:32:05,880
Each drum extends back 25 to 30 feet,

490
00:32:05,881 --> 00:32:08,460
so you're only seeing the face of it right here.

491
00:32:08,461 --> 00:32:09,920
In a central control room,

492
00:32:09,921 --> 00:32:13,240
workers carefully monitor both the steam production process

493
00:32:13,241 --> 00:32:15,340
and the delivery system.

494
00:32:15,341 --> 00:32:19,220
We produce steam at 800 psi and a high temperature

495
00:32:19,221 --> 00:32:21,740
of 840 degrees.

496
00:32:21,741 --> 00:32:24,200
For the safety of the steam system unit,

497
00:32:24,201 --> 00:32:29,460
we reduce that pressure to 200 psi and 410 degrees,

498
00:32:29,461 --> 00:32:32,380
which we send out in the underground pipes.

499
00:32:32,381 --> 00:32:34,220
This is our steam mimic system.

500
00:32:34,221 --> 00:32:37,560
This system monitors over 105 miles of piping

501
00:32:37,561 --> 00:32:38,860
in the steam system.

502
00:32:38,861 --> 00:32:40,740
It monitors the pressure in the steam system.

503
00:32:40,741 --> 00:32:43,380
So if there is a problem in anywhere in the systems

504
00:32:43,381 --> 00:32:45,160
throughout Manhattan, we'll know immediately

505
00:32:45,161 --> 00:32:47,460
where that problem is, and we can dispatch crews there

506
00:32:47,461 --> 00:32:48,660
to correct the problem.

507
00:32:49,220 --> 00:32:53,220
Con Edison has been in the steam business since 1930.

508
00:32:53,221 --> 00:32:58,220
The underground pipe responsible for this explosion was laid in 1923.

509
00:32:58,221 --> 00:33:01,220
It was part of the pre-existing steam system Con Edison purchased

510
00:33:01,221 --> 00:33:06,220
that first began supplying steam to Manhattan customers in 1882.

511
00:33:08,220 --> 00:33:10,220
Does that mean steam is outdated?

512
00:33:11,220 --> 00:33:12,220
Not a chance.

513
00:33:12,221 --> 00:33:17,220
The Hearst Tower, considered technologically and environmentally state-of-the-art,

514
00:33:17,221 --> 00:33:19,080
is one of Con Edison's customers.

515
00:33:20,120 --> 00:33:22,160
We're in the basement of the Hearst Tower

516
00:33:22,161 --> 00:33:24,660
where the steam from the Con Edison plant

517
00:33:24,661 --> 00:33:26,980
comes in through the street in this pipe

518
00:33:26,981 --> 00:33:30,640
at 175 pounds per square inch.

519
00:33:31,320 --> 00:33:34,300
It then gets reduced through the control panel here

520
00:33:34,301 --> 00:33:35,560
to 60 pounds,

521
00:33:35,980 --> 00:33:38,900
ultimately getting reduced to 15 pounds,

522
00:33:38,901 --> 00:33:41,540
and it then supplies all the heat

523
00:33:41,541 --> 00:33:43,200
and hot water to the building.

524
00:33:44,020 --> 00:33:46,640
Inside the building's 10-story high atrium,

525
00:33:46,641 --> 00:33:50,340
There are no old-fashioned radiators or hot air vents.

526
00:33:51,120 --> 00:33:55,540
Instead, the heat derived from the steam flows in tubes under the tile flooring,

527
00:33:56,180 --> 00:33:59,020
creating a comfortable ambient temperature in the huge space.

528
00:33:59,540 --> 00:34:04,480
The steam that we use is calculated in these meters here that Con Edison comes to read.

529
00:34:04,740 --> 00:34:10,260
We're using far less steam than was originally projected due to the efficiency of our heating systems.

530
00:34:10,261 --> 00:34:14,060
While Con Ed boils water to provide steam heat,

531
00:34:14,061 --> 00:34:17,960
the goal of other companies is to turn steam back into water.

532
00:34:17,961 --> 00:34:22,800
This facility processes and packages a variety of water products,

533
00:34:22,801 --> 00:34:24,400
including distilled water.

534
00:34:24,401 --> 00:34:29,420
Distillation involves boiling water and vaporizing it into steam.

535
00:34:29,421 --> 00:34:33,620
Boiling sterilizes the water, and when the steam lifts out of it,

536
00:34:33,621 --> 00:34:36,060
salts and other solids are left behind.

537
00:34:36,061 --> 00:34:38,760
Then the steam is cooled,

538
00:34:38,761 --> 00:34:43,900
And when it condenses back into liquid form, it's as close to pure H2O as possible.

539
00:34:46,060 --> 00:34:50,060
While distillation can produce clean water from a very contaminated source,

540
00:34:50,500 --> 00:34:53,520
this process begins with a municipal water supply.

541
00:34:54,800 --> 00:34:59,580
It arrives in the pipe above, fairly directly from the Sierras, as it turns out.

542
00:34:59,980 --> 00:35:03,140
But we treat it before processing through the distilter.

543
00:35:03,760 --> 00:35:07,360
First, we run it through some sand filters to take our particulate matter.

544
00:35:07,361 --> 00:35:11,280
We run it through carbon filters to take out chlorine that might be present.

545
00:35:11,900 --> 00:35:14,660
And then we run it through softeners to take out the hardness.

546
00:35:15,440 --> 00:35:20,380
All this is done to provide a very clean water before we even enter the distillation process.

547
00:35:21,320 --> 00:35:24,240
To eliminate any contaminants picked up in the system,

548
00:35:24,920 --> 00:35:29,080
the distilled water runs through submicron filters and then into a tank,

549
00:35:29,400 --> 00:35:33,540
where ozone gas bubbles up through it to treat any remaining impurities.

550
00:35:34,400 --> 00:35:35,880
Now it's ready for bottling.

551
00:35:35,881 --> 00:35:39,120
This is about as per as you can get for water.

552
00:35:39,121 --> 00:35:42,120
It's half a part or so per million of dissolved solids.

553
00:35:42,121 --> 00:35:45,760
The water in the gallon containers is an all-purpose product

554
00:35:45,761 --> 00:35:48,200
that's recommended for use in steam irons.

555
00:35:48,201 --> 00:35:50,120
But compared to regular water,

556
00:35:50,121 --> 00:35:52,560
distilled water is relatively tasteless.

557
00:35:52,561 --> 00:35:55,840
So to bottle drinking water,

558
00:35:55,841 --> 00:35:59,800
these tanks add back brand-specific electrolytes and minerals.

559
00:35:59,801 --> 00:36:03,080
These actually make the water healthier to drink

560
00:36:03,081 --> 00:36:05,200
and give it a more appealing taste.

561
00:36:05,201 --> 00:36:14,280
While this plotting, mechanized oddity may look like it just walked off the pages of a Jules Verne novel,

562
00:36:14,760 --> 00:36:17,640
it's actually part of a trend known as steampunk.

563
00:36:18,860 --> 00:36:26,300
The term has been around since the late 1980s to label contemporary drawings and objects designed with a pseudo-Victorian bent.

564
00:36:27,880 --> 00:36:31,460
In his garage, in a quiet central California neighborhood,

565
00:36:31,461 --> 00:36:35,060
Yi Wei Huang began turning his steampunk art

566
00:36:35,061 --> 00:36:37,460
into a steam-powered reality.

567
00:36:37,461 --> 00:36:40,460
I've drawn steampunk kind of inspired art

568
00:36:40,461 --> 00:36:43,900
pretty much all my life, drawing old robots and stuff

569
00:36:43,901 --> 00:36:46,900
that uses old Victorian technology.

570
00:36:46,901 --> 00:36:49,820
And I put a lot of thought into how it might work,

571
00:36:49,821 --> 00:36:52,020
you know, and the technology behind it,

572
00:36:52,021 --> 00:36:56,420
so much so that I decided that it might actually work.

573
00:36:56,421 --> 00:37:00,020
Yi Wei, who makes a living working for a video game company,

574
00:37:00,021 --> 00:37:01,880
doesn't build his own parts.

575
00:37:02,920 --> 00:37:05,300
And the miniature steam engines he uses

576
00:37:05,301 --> 00:37:06,960
come from hobby stores.

577
00:37:07,800 --> 00:37:10,760
It's the unusual ways he cobbles everything together

578
00:37:10,761 --> 00:37:12,060
that grabs people's attention.

579
00:37:14,240 --> 00:37:17,680
This one actually won the best of show and robo games.

580
00:37:18,200 --> 00:37:19,800
It's in the shape of a trilobite,

581
00:37:19,980 --> 00:37:22,480
which is a prehistoric animal.

582
00:37:24,580 --> 00:37:26,480
Steam is very intriguing

583
00:37:26,481 --> 00:37:29,180
because you get to play with fire, you know,

584
00:37:29,181 --> 00:37:32,920
and you get to play with something that's a little bit dangerous, a little bit unusual.

585
00:37:33,220 --> 00:37:35,700
It's all visual. You can see it. You can smell it.

586
00:37:36,280 --> 00:37:38,060
So this is the steam rover.

587
00:37:38,800 --> 00:37:42,080
It's basically a steam engine powered.

588
00:37:42,480 --> 00:37:45,500
It's six-wheel drive, four-wheel steering, little steam machine.

589
00:37:46,900 --> 00:37:52,040
It's one of the more powerful ones that I have, and it's just a blast to drive around.

590
00:37:52,780 --> 00:37:56,000
Most of E-Way's engines sound like tiny locomotives,

591
00:37:56,180 --> 00:37:58,860
but this one makes a distinctly different sound.

592
00:37:59,180 --> 00:38:08,860
I decided to do a turbine since I've done so many piston driven steam machines.

593
00:38:08,861 --> 00:38:13,860
So it was a challenge finding a turbine that actually works well.

594
00:38:13,861 --> 00:38:18,800
It uses up a lot of steam very, very fast and has super high RPM but not a whole lot

595
00:38:18,801 --> 00:38:19,800
of torque.

596
00:38:19,801 --> 00:38:22,560
So a lot of gearing down is required.

597
00:38:22,561 --> 00:38:27,720
And the forward reverse is done by a clutch because the turbine only turns one way.

598
00:38:27,721 --> 00:38:31,720
This is the Steam Centipede. It's one of my most complex machines that I've built.

599
00:38:31,721 --> 00:38:32,941
It has 32 legs.

600
00:38:32,942 --> 00:38:38,720
In old science fiction movies, there's a lot of steam walking machines,

601
00:38:38,721 --> 00:38:42,874
but there's a reason why it's really hard to pull off.

602
00:38:42,875 --> 00:38:45,720
It's just not as efficient as wheels.

603
00:38:46,720 --> 00:38:51,720
I-Way has managed to get even his most complex designs up and running on a small scale.

604
00:38:51,721 --> 00:38:54,720
But what about a full-sized version?

605
00:38:54,721 --> 00:38:58,600
I would love to see one of these huge steam machines,

606
00:38:59,560 --> 00:39:01,660
something like this that can carry people around.

607
00:39:02,020 --> 00:39:05,220
But this already scares me that the boilers could fail

608
00:39:05,221 --> 00:39:10,040
on a big scale that's not something I want to think about.

609
00:39:11,240 --> 00:39:12,760
But in nearby Oakland,

610
00:39:13,200 --> 00:39:16,500
a group of artisans operating under the Kinetic Steamworks banner

611
00:39:16,501 --> 00:39:19,460
take on the challenge of dealing with fire and water

612
00:39:19,461 --> 00:39:21,000
on a much bigger scale,

613
00:39:21,520 --> 00:39:23,220
with sometimes startling results.

614
00:39:24,720 --> 00:39:30,440
Here at Kinetic Steamworks, we have a complete reverence for the vintage equipment and the

615
00:39:30,441 --> 00:39:35,340
stylings of the way things used to be done, but we really like to take the vintage equipment

616
00:39:35,341 --> 00:39:40,960
and then use it for contemporary art pieces.

617
00:39:40,961 --> 00:39:46,060
Like their 1920 traction engine named Hortense, that provided the steam power for a belt drive

618
00:39:46,061 --> 00:39:48,220
that turned a carousel.

619
00:39:48,221 --> 00:39:50,320
There were actually steam carousels back in the day.

620
00:39:50,321 --> 00:39:56,480
This was a little bit more on an industrial scale, and it really was a mass of belt movement

621
00:39:56,481 --> 00:40:02,000
and gear motion, reciprocating motion that was much of a pleasure to ride the carousel

622
00:40:02,001 --> 00:40:07,900
as it was to watch the entire operation.

623
00:40:07,901 --> 00:40:12,960
At the Kinetic Steamworks 6,000-square-foot shop, the group fires up a small boiler to

624
00:40:12,961 --> 00:40:15,820
supply steam to some of their smaller projects.

625
00:40:15,821 --> 00:40:22,340
The boiler is called the blister by some and little baby steamy pants by some others.

626
00:40:22,341 --> 00:40:28,300
We have a firebox and right now we're burning liquid fuel and wood.

627
00:40:28,301 --> 00:40:30,580
Here comes some steam.

628
00:40:30,581 --> 00:40:39,580
This is a mock-up of what might have been used in a factory at the turn of the century,

629
00:40:39,581 --> 00:40:44,300
the last century, not the closest one, where you've got steam coming to a small engine

630
00:40:44,301 --> 00:40:51,300
like this and there's a PTO, a power takeoff here that the sewing machine operator can

631
00:40:51,301 --> 00:40:53,324
tension this belt or detension it.

632
00:40:53,325 --> 00:40:56,300
It just basically takes steam power, turns it into

633
00:40:56,301 --> 00:41:01,300
centrifugal force and makes the flywheel on the sewing machine work.

634
00:41:01,301 --> 00:41:05,843
Not everything we do here is practical at Kinetic Steamworks.

635
00:41:05,844 --> 00:41:08,300
However, art can be a lot of fun.

636
00:41:08,301 --> 00:41:11,931
This is a sculpture that I put together out of old musical instruments.

637
00:41:11,932 --> 00:41:12,800
One of the things

638
00:41:12,801 --> 00:41:16,300
that we as an organization did was restore a steamboat,

639
00:41:16,301 --> 00:41:18,600
a paddle wheel, stern wheel steamboat,

640
00:41:18,601 --> 00:41:21,280
and it was a beautiful experience.

641
00:41:21,281 --> 00:41:24,240
The artist that we collaborated with, Swoon,

642
00:41:24,241 --> 00:41:27,820
she decorated our vessel, and despite the fact

643
00:41:27,821 --> 00:41:30,620
that it didn't necessarily look like a steamboat

644
00:41:30,621 --> 00:41:33,660
of the 1800s, it ran like one.

645
00:41:33,661 --> 00:41:36,620
The steamboat now sits quietly in the back of the shop

646
00:41:36,621 --> 00:41:41,320
while the Steamworks gang is dreaming up their next project.

647
00:41:41,321 --> 00:41:44,520
In the meantime, let's just blow off a little steam.

